WEBVTT

00:00:00.850 --> 00:00:14.330
<v Ira Glass>OK, Sarah. So it's ten to 1:00 on a Friday night in November, and we're sitting on your porch. And it seems pretty quiet.
<v Ira Glass>好的，莎拉。现在是11月一个周五的晚上，快凌晨1点钟了，我们坐在你家的门廊上。看起来挺安静的。

00:00:14.330 --> 00:00:15.480
<v Sarah Koenig>It's not that bad.
<v Sarah Koenig>其实没那么糟。

00:00:15.480 --> 00:00:32.270
<v Ira Glass>One of the producers of our radio show, Sarah Koenig, moved to a college town because her husband got a job teaching there. And by day, her neighborhood is like a college town that you would see in an old Hollywood film. Beautiful professors' homes built in the '20s and '30s. Tree-lined streets. Gardens.
<v Ira Glass>我们广播节目的制片人之一，莎拉·凯尼格，搬到了一个大学城，因为她丈夫在那里得到了一份教职。白天，她所在的街区就像你在好莱坞老电影里看到的大学城一样。建于20世纪20和30年代漂亮的教授住宅，绿树成荫的街道，还有花园。

00:00:32.270 --> 00:00:36.470
<v Ira Glass>But by night, we'd been on her porch for five minutes when--
<v Ira Glass>但到了晚上，我们在她门廊才坐了五分钟，就——

00:00:36.470 --> 00:00:46.350
<v Sarah  Koenig>They seem drunk. These kids seem drunk. This couple here, they're sort of staggering a bit. I think he's like holding her up or something.
<v Sarah  Koenig>他们看起来喝醉了。这些孩子看起来喝醉了。这儿有对情侣，他们走路有点踉踉跄跄的。我觉得男的在扶着女的，或者别的什么。

00:00:46.350 --> 00:00:50.430
<v Ira Glass>It's nine students walking down the middle of the street. Happens all the time.
<v Ira Glass>那是九个学生走在街道中间。这种事经常发生。

00:00:50.430 --> 00:01:04.710
<v Sarah  Koenig>It's noisy, right? It's like it doesn't occur to them at all, I think, that there are people just in these houses trying to sleep. And I kind of remember being that way in college, too, actually.
<v Sarah  Koenig>很吵，对吧？我想他们根本就没意识到，这些房子里还有人正试图睡觉。其实，我记得我在上大学时也这样。

00:01:04.710 --> 00:01:05.750
<v Ira Glass>Yeah, I do too.
<v Ira Glass>是的，我也是。

00:01:05.750 --> 00:01:10.490
<v Ira Glass>Six minutes later, there's another group of drunk students. One tosses something onto a lawn.
<v Ira Glass>六分钟后，又是另一群喝醉的学生。其中一个往草坪上扔了什么东西。

00:01:10.490 --> 00:01:19.230
<v Sarah Koenig>Can you guys pick up your trash, please? Can you pick up your plate, your pizza plate, please? People live here.
<v Sarah Koenig>嘿，你们能把垃圾捡起来吗？能把那个盘子，那个披萨盘捡起来吗？有人住在这儿。

00:01:19.230 --> 00:01:21.250
<v Sarah Koenig>They pay no attention.
<v Sarah Koenig>他们根本不理会。

00:01:21.250 --> 00:01:26.120
<v Ira Glass>Five minutes after that, we hear this clattering from the alley that runs next to Sarah's house.
<v Ira Glass>五分钟后，我们听到莎拉家旁边的巷子里传来一阵叮当声。

00:01:26.120 --> 00:01:30.170
<v Sarah Koenig>Whoa, what was that? Actually, I'd kind of like to know what that was.
<v Sarah Koenig>哇，那是什么？其实，我还真想知道那是什么。

00:01:30.170 --> 00:01:31.220
<v Sarah Koenig>What's going on?
<v Sarah Koenig>怎么回事？

00:01:31.220 --> 00:01:38.440
<v Ira Glass>We run down the alley. Two college boys run away as they see us coming. Don't go back there, you could get raped, they yell to Sarah.
<v Ira Glass>我们跑进巷子。两个大学生看到我们过来就跑了。别去那儿，会被强奸的，他们对莎拉喊道。

00:01:38.440 --> 00:01:45.310
<v Sarah  Koenig>See, OK. They get threw somebody's trashcan, or like drop kicked it up in the air.
<v Sarah  Koenig>看吧，好吧。他们把别人的垃圾桶撞翻了，或者说是踢飞到了空中。

00:01:45.310 --> 00:01:52.060
<v Ira Glass>12 minutes after that, from the other direction, we hear a scraping and a loud rumbling, I guess.
<v Ira Glass>12分钟后，从另一个方向，我们听到一阵摩擦声和巨大的轰鸣声，我想是这样。

00:01:52.060 --> 00:01:57.140
<v Sarah Koenig>That's somebody's property. That might have been a sign, actually.
<v Sarah Koenig>那是别人的财产。那可能是一个标志牌，真的。

00:01:57.140 --> 00:02:10.330
<v Ira Glass>I don't know how many street signs you need to hear dragged through your neighborhood before you can recognize the sound it makes from all the way around the corner, but apparently my colleague Sarah has seen whatever that number is. We go to investigate and find two guys.
<v Ira Glass>我不知道在你能从拐角处辨认出街道标志被拖行时发出的声音之前，需要听到多少个标志牌被拖行，但显然我的同事莎拉已经见识过了那个数字是多少。我们去调查，发现有两个家伙。

00:02:10.330 --> 00:02:12.010
<v Ira Glass>So just describe what you've got here.
<v Ira Glass>描述一下你们这儿有什么。

00:02:12.010 --> 00:02:13.400
<v Male Student 1>A stop sign.
<v Male Student 1>一个停车标志。

00:02:13.400 --> 00:02:15.700
<v Male Student 2>Nice big pole on it.
<v Male Student 2>上面还有根又大又漂亮的柱子。

00:02:15.700 --> 00:02:19.330
<v Sarah  Koenig>A stop sign. That's kind of a big thing when you see it up close, isn't it?
<v Sarah  Koenig>一个停车标志。近看这玩意儿挺大个的，不是吗？

00:02:19.330 --> 00:02:20.510
<v Male Student 1>Sorry.
<v Male Student 1>抱歉。

00:02:20.510 --> 00:02:22.570
<v Sarah Koenig>Where did you find that? Like, where does it belong?
<v Sarah Koenig>你们从哪儿发现它的？它本该在哪儿？

00:02:22.570 --> 00:02:23.790
<v Male Student 1>I don't know.
<v Male Student 1>我不知道。

00:02:23.790 --> 00:02:25.040
<v Male Student 2>That is a good question.
<v Male Student 2>问得好。

00:02:25.040 --> 00:02:27.060
<v Male Student 1>We just found it on a lawn. We found it on a lawn.
<v Male Student 1>我们刚在草坪上发现的。我们是在草坪上找到的。

00:02:27.060 --> 00:02:28.210
<v Sarah Koenig>Oh, you found it on someone's lawn.
<v Sarah Koenig>噢，你们在别人的草坪上发现的。

00:02:28.210 --> 00:02:29.480
<v Male Student 2>It was already ripped off.
<v Male Student 2>它本来就被扯下来了。

00:02:29.480 --> 00:02:30.980
<v Male Student 1>Yeah, it was already [BLEEP].
<v Male Student 1>是啊，它本来就[消音]了。

00:02:30.980 --> 00:02:33.470
<v Male Student 2>We just decided, hey, why not make use of it and take it?
<v Male Student 2>我们只是觉得，嘿，为什么不利用一下把它带走呢？

00:02:33.470 --> 00:02:38.690
<v Ira Glass>Of course, this story makes no sense at all. I inform the two guys that I don't need to know their names.
<v Ira Glass>当然，这故事完全讲不通。我告诉那两个家伙，我不需要知道他们的名字。

00:02:38.690 --> 00:02:40.700
<v Male Student 2>Well, in that case, we took it from the corner right there.
<v Male Student 2>好吧，既然这样，我们是从那边的拐角处拿的。

00:02:40.700 --> 00:02:42.830
<v Sarah Koenig>Which one?
<v Sarah Koenig>哪一个？

00:02:42.830 --> 00:02:45.880
<v Male Student 2>Where that car just turned. Pretty sure.
<v Male Student 2>刚才那辆车转弯的地方。我很确定。

00:02:45.880 --> 00:02:46.810
<v Ira Glass>Pulled it out of the ground?
<v Ira Glass>从地里拔出来的？

00:02:46.810 --> 00:02:53.390
<v Male Student 2>Yeah, it was cemented in pretty well. Just get a little rocking back and forth between two people, comes right up.
<v Male Student 2>对，它水泥封得很牢。两个人来回晃几下，就出来了。

00:02:53.390 --> 00:02:56.950
<v Ira Glass>Did you guys come from like a house party somewhere?
<v Ira Glass>你们是从哪里的家庭派对过来的吗？

00:02:56.950 --> 00:03:01.630
<v Male Student 2>No. We were just hanging out with a bunch of friends, just drinking. Taking some shots.
<v Male Student 2>不，我们只是和一群朋友待在一起，喝酒，喝了几杯。

00:03:01.630 --> 00:03:05.870
<v Ira Glass>Seems like kind of an academic distinction to me, but.
<v Ira Glass>对我来说这似乎是一个学术上的区别，但是。

00:03:05.870 --> 00:03:23.220
<v Ira Glass>With that, the other guy starts running with the street sign toward Garner Street, which is hard, because it's attached to a seven foot metal pole, and it's heavy. Sarah and I go back around the corner to her front yard. The evening is not over at all. Just in time to spot another student, this one coming out of a garden. Sarah assumes from the bushes back there.
<v Ira Glass>说完，另一个人开始扛着路标朝加纳街跑去，这很困难，因为它连着一根七英尺高的金属杆，而且很重。莎拉和我转回拐角处，回到她的前院。夜晚根本还没结束。刚好撞见另一个学生，这家伙正从花园里出来。莎拉推测他是从后面的灌木丛里出来的。

00:03:23.220 --> 00:03:24.750
<v Male Student 3>I didn't pee.
<v Male Student 3>我没撒尿。

00:03:24.750 --> 00:03:26.610
<v Sarah Koenig>Really? Really?
<v Sarah Koenig>真的吗？真的？

00:03:26.610 --> 00:03:27.790
<v Male Student 3>I was just sitting down.
<v Male Student 3>我只是坐下来而已。

00:03:27.790 --> 00:03:36.670
<v Ira Glass>He goes. And it's not a minute later that Sara points toward the alley, where there are three girls in miniskirts, under a streetlight, fully visible.
<v Ira Glass>他走了。没过一分钟，莎拉指向巷子，那里有三个穿着迷你裙的女孩，在路灯下，清晰可见。

00:03:36.670 --> 00:03:39.900
<v Sarah Koenig>Peeing, peeing, peeing, peeing, peeing. [INAUDIBLE]
<v Sarah Koenig>撒尿，撒尿，撒尿，撒尿，撒尿。[听不清]

00:03:39.900 --> 00:03:42.450
<v Ira Glass>One girl hikes up her skirt.
<v Ira Glass>一个女孩提起裙子。

00:03:42.450 --> 00:03:43.800
<v Ira Glass>Get back, get back, get back.
<v Ira Glass>退后，退后，退后。

00:03:43.800 --> 00:03:53.300
<v Sarah Koenig>But they're peeing in my yard. They're peeing in my yard. That's my car, OK, and three feet back is a girl's white ass, peeing.
<v Sarah Koenig>可她们在我的院子里撒尿。她们在我的院子里撒尿。那是我的车，好吗，三英尺外就是一个女孩的白屁股，在撒尿。

00:03:53.300 --> 00:03:58.340
<v Sarah Koenig>She saw we saw her. She stopped. She stopped.
<v Sarah Koenig>她看到我们看见她了。她停下了。她停下了。

00:03:58.340 --> 00:04:02.190
<v Sarah Koenig>You know, that might be why the plants grow really, really well in that spot, I'm just realizing.
<v Sarah Koenig>你知道，我刚意识到，这可能就是为什么那个地方的植物长得特别特别好的原因。

00:04:02.190 --> 00:04:08.900
<v Ira Glass>Sarah's caught other groups of girls peeing in that same spot. Once she heard a girl say, "This is a good place. I go here all the time."
<v Ira Glass>莎拉抓到过其他群体在同一个地方撒尿。有一次她听到一个女孩说：“这地方不错。我常来这儿。”

00:04:08.900 --> 00:04:13.520
<v Sarah Koenig>They're so embarrassed. There's like, muffled giggling happening across the street.
<v Sarah Koenig>她们好尴尬。街对面传来闷闷的吃吃笑声。

00:04:13.520 --> 00:04:38.410
<v Ira Glass>The college town that Sarah lives in is State College, Pennsylvania, home of Penn State University, which this year was named the number one party school in the country by the people who name the number one party school, the Princeton Review. And this is apparently what it means to be the number one party school. From the moment that Sarah and I turned on the tape recorder to the moment that she sent those girls looking for somebody else's yard to pee in, it's only been 34 minutes. It's 1:30 in the morning.
<v Ira Glass>莎拉居住的大学城是宾夕法尼亚州的州立学院，宾夕法尼亚州立大学所在地。今年，它被评选为全国第一派对学校，评选机构是普林斯顿评论。显然，这就是成为第一派对学校的含义。从莎拉和我按下录音机的那一刻起，到她把那些女孩赶去别人的院子撒尿，才过了34分钟。现在是凌晨1点半。

00:04:38.410 --> 00:04:57.730
<v Sarah Koenig>The fact that we saw that much mayhem sort of going on, at one corner-- so that means it's, multiply that by, you know, this entire neighborhood and other neighborhoods around-- people are peeing everywhere, garbage cans are getting kicked, stop signs are getting pulled out of the road, people are littering. You know.
<v Sarah Koenig>我们亲眼目睹了这么多混乱在发生，在一个拐角——这意味着乘以整个街区，以及周围的其他街区——到处都有人在撒尿，垃圾桶被踢，停车标志被拔出路面，人们到处乱扔垃圾。你知道的。

00:04:57.730 --> 00:05:18.900
<v Ira Glass>The Princeton Review chooses the number one party school from online student surveys. 120,000 students at 371 schools around the country answered these questions. "How widely used are beer, alcohol, marijuana, and other drugs at your school?" "How big is frat life at your school?" And finally, "How many hours do you study each day?"
<v Ira Glass>普林斯顿评论是通过在线学生调查选出第一派对学校的。全国371所学校的12万名学生回答了这些问题。“你们学校啤酒、酒精、大麻和其他毒品的使用有多广泛？”“你们学校的兄弟会生活有多盛大？”最后，“你们每天学习多少小时？”

00:05:18.900 --> 00:05:32.330
<v Ira Glass>Students aren't randomly selected, so it's not, strictly speaking, a scientific survey. But the last two years, Penn State has done very well. It was the number three party school last year, number six the year before that, and nobody argues it is not a very big school for partying.
<v Ira Glass>学生不是随机抽选的，所以严格来说，这不是一项科学调查。但过去两年，宾州立大学表现非常好。去年是第三派对学校，前年是第六，没人会争辩说这不是一所狂欢的大校。

00:05:32.330 --> 00:05:38.940
<v Graham Spanier>Well, look. We've never said we don't want our students to have fun.
<v Graham Spanier>听着。我们从没说过不希望学生们玩得开心。

00:05:38.940 --> 00:05:51.710
<v Ira Glass>The University's president, Graham Spanier, discussed the number one ranking in a faculty senate meeting this fall. The only recording that exist is from a webcast Penn State did at the meeting, so you hear the audio is little sketchy. But still fine.
<v Ira Glass>大学校长格雷厄姆·斯帕尼尔在秋季的一次教职工参议院会议上讨论了这一第一排名。唯一的录音来自宾州立大学在该会议上的网络广播，所以音频听起来有点粗糙。但还可以。

00:05:51.710 --> 00:06:03.680
<v Graham Spanier>I mean, when people talk about oh, Penn State's a party school, I'm not-- it doesn't bother me at all, actually. I wish we weren't ranked number one.
<v Graham Spanier>我是说，当人们谈论噢，宾州立大学是派对学校时，我不——这其实一点也不困扰我。我只是希望我们没有被排在第一。

00:06:03.680 --> 00:06:40.990
<v Graham Spanier>You know why we're ranked number one? Have we talked about this? You know why we're ranked number one? The students vote! It's not like somebody came in here and did an assessment of the place. It's an online, web-based thing. And whenever there's an online, web-based thing, Penn State always wins. We have the best mascot in the country. We probably do. But I guarantee that any online voting for the best mascot in the country, we're going to win that. You know? We're the number one university on Facebook. We're the number one university with Twitter.
<v Graham Spanier>你知道为什么我们被排在第一吗？我们谈过这个吗？你知道为什么我们被排在第一吗？是学生投的票！又不是有人亲自过来评估这个地方。这是一个基于网络的在线投票。只要是在线投票，宾州立大学总是赢。我们有全国最好的吉祥物。可能确实如此。但我敢保证，任何关于全国最佳吉祥物的在线投票，我们都会赢。你知道吗？我们在Facebook上是第一大学。我们在Twitter上是第一大学。

00:06:40.990 --> 00:06:57.570
<v Graham Spanier>So when these party school things, some of our students get online and vote? Of course they want to be voted number one. Yeah, Penn State. Then I have to clean up the mess after the votes are in. Because I got all the donors and alumni, and the media I'm calling for comment.
<v Graham Spanier>所以当这些派对学校评选出现时，我们的学生就会上网投票？当然，他们想被选为第一。是的，宾州立大学。然后我必须在投票结果出来后清理烂摊子。因为我得应付所有的捐赠者、校友，还有媒体打电话来要评论。

00:06:57.570 --> 00:07:07.920
<v Ira Glass>The amount of drinking at the school does concern Spanier. He told the faculty that when people ask him what's the biggest problem at Penn State, they always expect him to say money, but the answer for a long time has been alcohol.
<v Ira Glass>学校里的饮酒量确实让斯帕尼尔感到担忧。他告诉教职员工，当人们问他宾州立大学最大的问题是什么时，他们总是期望他说钱，但长期以来的答案一直是酒精。

00:07:07.920 --> 00:07:13.860
<v Ira Glass>And he said it's a problem that's bigger than Penn State. Nearly a third of high school seniors across the country binge drink.
<v Ira Glass>他说这是一个比宾州立大学更大的问题。全国近三分之一的高中毕业生酗酒。

00:07:13.860 --> 00:07:24.020
<v Graham Spanier>So it's a bit of a problem that we inherit. But we also have a cultural environment here, an atmosphere that fosters it.
<v Graham Spanier>所以这是一个我们继承下来的小问题。但我们这里也有一个文化环境，一种助长它的氛围。

00:07:24.020 --> 00:07:41.910
<v Ira Glass>Well, today we devote our entire show to the nation's number one party school. Not because it's number one and what happens here is unusual, but because it's not unusual at all. Lots of schools, especially a lot of big state schools, do this much partying.
<v Ira Glass>今天，我们将整个节目献给全国第一派对学校。不是因为它排第一，也不是因为这里发生的事情不同寻常，而是因为它一点也不寻常。很多学校，尤其是很多大型州立大学，都有这么多的派对活动。

00:07:41.910 --> 00:07:57.780
<v Ira Glass>Five of us from our staff went down to State College where Sarah lives to record one football weekend in November. What we bring you today is a travelogue from this place where the rules and norms are probably very different from where you live right now. Its own rituals, its own jargon, its own native dress. We wanted to see what it was all about.
<v Ira Glass>我们五名员工去了莎拉居住的州立学院，录制了11月的一个橄榄球周末。我们今天带给大家的是一个来自这个地方的游记，这里的规则和规范可能和你现在居住的地方非常不同。它有自己的仪式，自己的行话，自己的本地服装。我们想看看这到底是怎么回事。

00:07:57.780 --> 00:08:26.720
<v Ira Glass>Our show today, in four acts. Only three of them actually pre-soaked in vodka. From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life, distributed by Public Radio International. I'm Ira Glass. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>我们今天的节目分四幕。只有其中三幕实际上是预先浸泡在伏特加里的。来自芝加哥WBEZ，这是《美国生活》，由公共广播国际发行。我是艾拉·格拉斯。请继续收听。

00:08:26.720 --> 00:08:30.890
<v Ira Glass>Act One. I'm Not As Think As You Drunk I Am.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕。我没醉到那种地步。

00:08:30.890 --> 00:08:49.380
<v Ira Glass>Let's start with the drinking, which is epic. The Harvard School of Public Health did a study of what schools tend to have the heaviest drinking, and Penn State actually hits all the criteria that they found. It's located in the northeast, and it has a large undergraduate population-- that would be, in this case, 44,000 students-- a large fraternity system, a nationally-known football team.
<v Ira Glass>让我们从饮酒开始，这非常疯狂。哈佛公共卫生学院做了一项关于哪些学校倾向于最严重酗酒的研究，宾州立大学实际上符合他们发现的所有标准。它位于东北部，拥有大量的本科生群体——本案中是44,000名学生——庞大的兄弟会系统，还有一支全国知名的橄榄球队。

00:08:49.380 --> 00:09:03.470
<v Ira Glass>Now, the fact is that it probably doesn't hurt it's isolated. State College is in a valley nestled between two mountain ridges in a state forest. People here call it "Happy Valley." It's surrounded by farms. You're in the middle of nowhere, a few people said, without much to do but drink.
<v Ira Glass>事实上，它很孤立可能没帮上忙。州立学院位于州森林中两条山脊之间的一个山谷里。这里的人称它为“快乐谷”。周围都是农场。有人说，你置身于荒郊野外，除了喝酒没别的事做。

00:09:03.470 --> 00:09:08.980
<v Ira Glass>And to talk about this drinking, I'm joined now in the studio by Sarah Koenig. Because Sarah, you recorded these first couple clips of tape.
<v Ira Glass>关于这个饮酒问题，我现在在演播室里有莎拉·凯尼格加入。因为莎拉，你录制了最初的那几段录音。

00:09:08.980 --> 00:09:31.700
<v Sarah Koenig>Right. A couple of weeks before you guys came to State College, there was this huge freak snowstorm, and they had to cancel tailgating for the first time anyone could remember because the conditions were so dangerous by the field. But it didn't stop anyone. I saw people tailgating in their yards, on their front lawns. Even in my neighborhood, I saw people under, like, these building underpasses.
<v Sarah Koenig>对。在你们来州立学院的前几周，发生了一场可怕的暴风雪，他们不得不取消了大家记忆中第一次的汽车后备箱派对，因为赛场周边的环境太危险了。但并没有阻止任何人。我看到人们在他们的院子里，在前草坪上举行派对。甚至在我家附近，我看到人们在像建筑通道下这样的地方。

