<v Female    Student   1>Thank you so much for the drink!
<v Female    Student   1>非常感谢你的这杯酒！

00:11:20.340 --> 00:11:41.100
<v Ira   Glass>At tailgate parties, you see just how deeply embedded drinking is in life at Penn State. It's entire families. It's several generations together. It's outdoor, public drinking, which gives you the feeling, when you're there, that the whole world is drinking. Probably because there's so much of it.
<v Ira   Glass>在派对上，你可以看到饮酒在宾州立大学的生活中有多深厚。那是整个家庭。是好几代人在一起。这是户外的、公开的饮酒，当你身处其中时，会让你觉得全世界都在喝酒。可能是因为这种事太多了。

00:11:41.100 --> 00:11:57.150
<v Ira   Glass>Penn State now has the largest stadium in North America. It holds over 100,000 people. The tailgates are these huge masses of humanity, spread out as far as you can see, all around the stadium. Starting at eight in the morning, starting really early, which is when these alums got here. They talked to one of our producers, Aaron Scott.
<v Ira   Glass>宾州立大学现在拥有北美最大的体育场。能容纳超过10万人。派对是巨大的人群，在你视线所及范围内铺开，分布在体育场周围。从早上8点开始，真的非常早，这就是那些校友到达的时间。他们和我们的制片人之一亚伦·斯科特聊了聊。

00:11:57.150 --> 00:12:01.970
<v Aaron Scott>So you went to bed, three or four, you got up, and you're drinking again?
<v Aaron Scott>所以你睡了三四个小时，然后起床，又开始喝了？

00:12:01.970 --> 00:12:02.610
<v Alumna>Hells, yeah.
<v Alumna>该死的，当然。

00:12:02.610 --> 00:12:04.190
<v Aaron Scott>Is it ever too early to drink?
<v Aaron Scott>喝酒会太早吗？

00:12:04.190 --> 00:12:05.020
<v Alumna>Never.
<v Alumna>绝不。

00:12:05.020 --> 00:12:12.980
<v Ira Glass>Eight in the morning means, by the way, that if it's a night game, they will tailgate for 12 hours, in the cold, in the freezing cold sometimes, before kickoff.
<v Ira Glass>顺便说一句，早上8点意味着，如果是晚上的比赛，他们会在寒冷中，有时是在零下的寒冷中，在开球前参加12个小时的派对。

00:12:12.980 --> 00:12:17.170
<v Sarah Koenig>I talked to a bunch of alums at a tailgate about what would happen if they weren't allowed to drink at all.
<v Sarah Koenig>我和一群校友谈论过，如果他们完全不被允许喝酒会怎样。

00:12:17.170 --> 00:12:28.250
<v Mark Johnson>I think there would be a revolt. I think there would be a huge, huge pushback from the alumni that donate a lot of money to this school to say that you can't have alcohol at a tailgate.
<v Mark Johnson>我想会发生反抗。我认为那些给学校捐赠大量钱财的校友会强烈反对，说你不能在派对上喝酒。

00:12:28.250 --> 00:12:31.260
<v Sarah Koenig>That's Mark Johnson, class of 1977.
<v Sarah Koenig>那是马克·约翰逊，1977届学生。

00:12:31.260 --> 00:12:42.030
<v Mark Johnson>It's just part of the culture. I mean, that's why this school sells 110,000 tickets for every-- you know, just, it's a part of it. It's just part of that tradition.
<v Mark Johnson>这只是文化的一部分。我是说，这就是为什么这所学校每场比赛都能卖出11万张票——你知道，这只是其中一部分。这只是传统的一部分。

00:12:42.030 --> 00:13:06.190
<v Ira Glass>Usually pounding down drinks at eight in the morning can be kind of, I don't know, bad. But the main thing about this kind of drinking, where it's tied in with tradition, and football, and family, and overwhelming sense of school spirit, is that it feels incredibly wholesome. The whole campus feels like a Chevy commercial. Welcoming, people friendly. [UNINTELLIGIBLE]
<v Ira Glass>通常早上8点猛灌酒可能，我不知道，很不好。但关于这种饮酒的主要特点是，它与传统、橄榄球、家庭以及那种压倒性的学校精神联系在一起，它感觉非常健康。整个校园感觉就像雪佛兰的广告。热情好客，人们很友好。[听不清]

00:13:06.190 --> 00:13:25.370
<v Ira Glass>The sense that drinking together is what we do spills over from the football games into the whole weekend. Into the week, too, actually. It's Friday night, it's the night before the football game, outside East Hall, the big freshman dorms. Dozens of kids are lined up for free shuttle buses that are going to take them across campus to the frats and parties on the other side.
<v Ira Glass>这种一起喝酒是我们生活方式的感觉从橄榄球比赛蔓延到整个周末。甚至实际上延伸到了平时。这是周五晚上，橄榄球比赛的前一天，在新生宿舍东大厅外。几十个孩子排队等着免费的穿梭巴士，带他们穿过校园去另一边的兄弟会和派对。

00:13:25.370 --> 00:13:37.140
<v Ira Glass>Two of our producers, Jane Feltes and Lisa Pollak, noticed that all the girls wore these ratty-looking hoodies and jackets over their dressup clothes. Somebody else told them support for this is "fracket," and wanted some girls to confirm that.
<v Ira Glass>我们的两位制片人，简·费尔特斯和丽莎·波拉克，注意到所有的女孩在礼服外面都穿着这种破烂的连帽衫和外套。别人告诉她们这叫“兄弟会外套”，想让一些女孩确认一下。

00:13:37.140 --> 00:13:40.220
<v Jane Feltes>Have you guys heard of something called "frackets"?
<v Jane Feltes>你们听说过叫“兄弟会外套”的东西吗？

